"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes \c{libprotobuf}, a C++ library for serializing and
parsing messages in the Protocol Buffers wire format. For the details of the
library interface refer to the comments in its public headers. For the format
itself see the \l{https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/encoding/ encoding
specification}.


\h1#intro|Introduction|

The \c{libprotobuf} library is a C++ implementation of the Protocol Buffers
wire format. A message on the wire is a sequence of fields, each a key
followed by a value. The key holds the field number and the wire type, which
says how the value is encoded. Which schema type the value has, such as a
nested message, is something that only the message definition in the schema
tells.

The library works at this level. The serializer writes each field with the
function of its schema type, and the parser moves from field to field and
reads each one with the function of its schema type. So a program speaks a
protocol with the schema as its guide and has no generated code to build.
The library also carries several messages in one stream, each preceded by
its size, with a pair of \c{iostream} classes.

The parser accepts every message that the encoding specification defines and
rejects everything else (see \l{#conformance Conformance} for details).

We begin with a tutorial introduction that builds a small program for
recording tracks, each a named series of points with the times at which we
reached them. Along the way we check our messages against
\c{protoc}, the schema compiler of the reference implementation. The
remaining chapters then cover the details that the tutorial passes over.


\h#intro-setup|Project Setup|

Our program is called \c{track}. Let's start by creating a new executable
project for it (here we assume our current working directory is \c{/tmp}):

\
$ bdep new -l c++ -t exe track
created new executable project track in /tmp/track/

$ cd track
\

To use \c{libprotobuf} in our project, we first edit the
\c{repositories.manifest} file found in the root directory of the project and
add the \c{libprotobuf} repository as a prerequisite:

\
role: prerequisite
location: https://github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libprotobuf.git#main
\

Next, we edit the \c{manifest} file (again, found in the root of our project)
and specify the dependency on \c{libprotobuf}:

\
depends: libprotobuf ^0.1.0-a.0.1
\

\N|The \c{-a.0.1} part of the constraint lets it match the snapshots of
\c{libprotobuf} that precede its \c{0.1.0} release, which are the only
versions available until then. Once \c{0.1.0} is released, the constraint
becomes \c{^0.1.0}.|

Finally, we import the library in \c{track/buildfile}. We won't be writing
tests in this tutorial, so we also remove \c{testscript} that came with the
project, here and in the \c{track/} directory:

\
$ cat track/buildfile

libs =
import libs += libprotobuf%lib{protobuf}

exe{track}: {hxx ixx txx cxx}{**} $libs

cxx.poptions =+ \"-I$out_root\" \"-I$src_root\"
\

\N|If these files are new to you, see
\l{https://build2.org/build2-toolchain/doc/build2-toolchain-intro.xhtml#guide-add-remove-deps
Adding and Removing Dependencies} in the \c{build2} toolchain introduction for
the details on each of them.|

With the dependency in place, let's initialize our project in a build
configuration:

\
$ bdep init -C @gcc cc config.cxx=g++ --create-host-config
initializing in project /tmp/track/
created configuration @gcc /tmp/track-gcc/ 1 target
    default,forwarded,auto-synchronized
fetching git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libprotobuf#main
    (prerequisite of dir:/tmp/track)
fetching pkg:cppget.org/stable (prerequisite of
    git:github.com/iw4x-x64-stage/libprotobuf#main)
...
synchronizing:
  new icu-tools/74.2.0+3 [/tmp/track-host/] (required by
    libicuuc)
  new libicuuc/74.2.0+3 (required by libprotobuf)
  new libprotobuf/0.1.0-a.0... (required by track)
  new track/0.1.0-a.0.19700101000000
\

As you can see, \c{libprotobuf} depends on \c{libicuuc}, the common library
of ICU, which checks that the values of string fields are valid UTF-8. ICU
builds its data with its own tools, a build-time dependency that goes into a
configuration for the host, which \c{--create-host-config} lets \c{bdep}
create without asking. We are now ready to write some code.


\h#intro-write|Writing a Message|

Here is the schema of a track in the Protocol Buffers language. We won't be
compiling it, but it is what our code follows and we will give it to
\c{protoc} to check our messages:

\
$ cat ../track.proto

syntax = \"proto3\";

message Point
{
  sint32 x = 1;
  sint32 y = 2;
}

message Track
{
  string name = 1;
  repeated Point points = 2;
  repeated uint32 times = 3;
}
\

We will get to the times later. The first version of our program writes a
track with a name and three points to its standard output:

\
$ cat track/track.cxx

#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

#include <libprotobuf/serializer.hxx>

using namespace std;

struct point
{
  int32_t x;
  int32_t y;
};

int
main ()
{
  const vector<point> ps {{0, 0}, {3, -4}, {-120, 75}};

  string m;
  protobuf::serializer s (m);

  s.string (1, \"morning run\");

  for (const point& p: ps)
  {
    s.begin_message (2);
    s.sint32 (1, p.x);
    s.sint32 (2, p.y);
    s.end_message ();
  }

  print (\"{}\", m);
}
\

The \c{protobuf::serializer} class is declared in
\c{<libprotobuf/serializer.hxx>} and appends the message to the string that
we give it. Everything in the library is in the \c{protobuf} namespace. For
each field we call the function of its schema type with the field number and
the value, so \c{string()} for the name and \c{sint32()} for the
coordinates. A point is a nested message, which we write between
\c{begin_message()} and \c{end_message()}. Note that the length of a nested
message comes before it on the wire, so \c{end_message()} is where the
serializer writes it.

Let's build our program and look at what it writes:

\
$ b
c++ track/cxx{track} -> ../track-gcc/track/track/obje{track}
c++ ~/.build2/cache/src/libprotobuf-.../libprotobuf/cxx{parser}
    -> ...
...
ld ../track-gcc/libprotobuf-.../libprotobuf/libs{protobuf}
ld ../track-gcc/track/track/exe{track}
ln ../track-gcc/track/track/exe{track} -> track/

$ track/track | xxd
00000000: 0a0b 6d6f 726e 696e 6720 7275 6e12 0408  ..morning run...
00000010: 0010 0012 0408 0610 0712 0608 ef01 1096  ................
00000020: 01                                       .
\

The message starts with \c{0a}, the key of field 1 with the len wire type
(the field number shifted left by three bits with the wire type, 2, in the
low three bits), then the length of the name, \c{0b}, and the name itself.
Next comes the first point: \c{12} is the key of field 2, again len, and
\c{04} the length of the nested message, \c{08 00 10 00}, which holds the
two coordinates as varints.

Let's see what \c{protoc} makes of it. Its \c{--decode_raw} option decodes a
message without a schema, so it only shows the field numbers and the values
as the wire types carry them:

\
$ track/track | protoc --decode_raw
1: \"morning run\"
2 {
  1: 0
  2: 0
}
2 {
  1: 6
  2: 7
}
2 {
  1: 239
  2: 150
}
\

Notice that the coordinates are not the ones that we wrote. The \c{sint32}
schema type encodes its values with ZigZag, which maps the signed values
onto the unsigned ones alternately (0 to 0, -1 to 1, 1 to 2, etc), so that a
value of a small magnitude takes few bytes whatever its sign. So 3 becomes 6
and -4 becomes 7. The \c{int32} schema type writes the two's complement
instead, which takes ten bytes for any negative value (see
\l{#types Schema Types} for details).


\h#intro-read|Reading a Message|

Let's now teach our program to read its own messages. With the \c{read}
argument it parses the message on its standard input and prints the track:

\
$ cat track/track.cxx

#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <iterator>
#include <string_view>

#include <libprotobuf/parser.hxx>
#include <libprotobuf/serializer.hxx>

using namespace std;

struct point
{
  int32_t x;
  int32_t y;
};

void
write ()
{
  ... // As before.
}

point
read_point (protobuf::parser p)
{
  point r {0, 0};

  while (p.next ())
  {
    switch (p.field ())
    {
      case 1: r.x = p.sint32 (); break;
      case 2: r.y = p.sint32 (); break;
    }
  }

  return r;
}

void
read ()
{
  const string m (istreambuf_iterator<char> (cin), {});

  protobuf::parser p (m);

  while (p.next ())
  {
    switch (p.field ())
    {
      case 1:
      {
        println (\"name: {}\", p.string ());
        break;
      }
      case 2:
      {
        const point pt (read_point (p.message ()));
        println (\"point: {} {}\", pt.x, pt.y);
        break;
      }
    }
  }
}

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  try
  {
    if (argc > 1 && argv[1] == string_view (\"read\"))
      read ();
    else
      write ();
  }
  catch (const protobuf::invalid_input& e)
  {
    println (cerr, \"error: {}\", e.what ());
    println (cerr, \"  info: at offset {}\", e.position);
    return 1;
  }
}
\

The \c{protobuf::parser} class is declared in \c{<libprotobuf/parser.hxx>}.
Its \c{next()} function moves to the next field and returns \c{false} at the
end of the message, \c{field()} returns the number of the current field, and
the functions of the schema types return its value. The parser refers to
the message, so \c{string()} returns a \c{std::string_view} into it. For a
nested message, \c{message()} returns another parser, which we hand to
\c{read_point()}. Note that a field that we don't look at is skipped over,
and so is a field that our schema doesn't know.

Let's give it a try:

\
$ track/track | track/track read
name: morning run
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
point: -120 75
\

That a field we don't know is skipped is what lets a schema grow: a newer
writer can add fields that an older reader ignores. Let's pretend that a
newer version of our program adds field 4 with the value 42 at the end:

\
$ (track/track; printf '\\x20\\x2a') | track/track read
name: morning run
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
point: -120 75
\

A malformed message, on the other hand, is an error. The parser throws
\c{protobuf::invalid_input} with the description of the fault and the
position of the field that it concerns. Let's cut our message short and
then try a name that is not UTF-8, which a string must be:

\
$ track/track | head -c 30 | track/track read
name: morning run
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
error: incomplete len value in field 2
  info: at offset 25

$ printf '\\x0a\\x02\\xc3\\x28' | track/track read
error: invalid UTF-8 in string field 1
  info: at offset 0
\

As you can see, the parser reads each field as we get to it, so we print the
first points before it finds that the third one is incomplete. A program
that must not act on a part of a message would parse all of it before doing
anything with it.

Our program also reads what \c{protoc} writes, which we can check with its
\c{--encode} option. It takes the message in the text format and writes it in
the wire format:

\
$ echo 'name: \"evening walk\" points {x: 10 y: -10} times: [0, 30]' | \\
  protoc --proto_path=.. --encode=Track ../track.proto | \\
  track/track read
name: evening walk
point: 10 -10
\

Our program skips the times, since it doesn't know them yet. Let's fix
that.


\h#intro-repeated|Repeated Fields|

A repeated field is written as one field per element. For a repeated field
of a scalar type other than \c{string} and \c{bytes}, the writer can also
pack the elements into a single len field, and in the \c{proto3} syntax that
is what the schema calls for unless told otherwise. So the times of our
track are packed. Here are the changes to our program that write and read
them:

\
void
write ()
{
  const vector<point> ps {{0, 0}, {3, -4}, {-120, 75}};
  const vector<uint32_t> ts {0, 60, 125};

  ...

  s.packed_uint32 (3, ts);

  print (\"{}\", m);
}

void
read ()
{
  ...
  vector<uint32_t> ts;

  while (p.next ())
  {
    switch (p.field ())
    {
      ...
      case 3:
      {
        p.uint32 (ts);
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  println (\"times: {}\", ts);
}
\

The serializer writes a packed field with the \c{packed_*()} functions,
which take the elements as a \c{std::span}. On the reading side, the
overloads of the schema type functions that take a \c{std::vector} append
the value of the current field to it. A reader must accept a repeated field
in both forms, so these functions append one element if the field is not
packed and all of them if it is.

\
$ track/track | track/track read
name: morning run
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
point: -120 75
times: [0, 60, 125]

$ track/track | protoc --decode_raw | tail -1
3: \"\\000<}\"

$ echo 'name: \"evening walk\" points {x: 10 y: -10} times: [0, 30]' | \\
  protoc --proto_path=.. --encode=Track ../track.proto | \\
  track/track read
name: evening walk
point: 10 -10
times: [0, 30]
\

Without the schema, \c{protoc} shows the packed field for what it is on the
wire, a len field whose bytes are the varints 0, 60 (the character \c{<}),
and 125 (\c{\}}). With the schema it reads our message the same as we do:

\
$ track/track | protoc --proto_path=.. --decode=Track ../track.proto
name: \"morning run\"
points {
}
points {
  x: 3
  y: -4
}
points {
  x: -120
  y: 75
}
times: 0
times: 60
times: 125
\

Notice that \c{protoc} doesn't print the coordinates of the first point. In
the \c{proto3} syntax a scalar field with the value 0 is the same as a field
that is not there, so \c{protoc} only writes and prints the fields with
other values. Our serializer writes every field that we give it, which a
reader takes the same way.


\h#intro-streams|Several Messages|

So far our program reads its whole standard input as one message. That is
because a message carries no length of its own: it takes up the rest of its
input. To keep several tracks in one file, we write each one preceded by its
size, which is the delimited form that the reference implementation reads
and writes as well. The \c{<libprotobuf/stream.hxx>} header provides two
stream classes for this: \c{protobuf::ostream} writes delimited messages to
another output stream and \c{protobuf::istream} reads them from another
input stream. Here are the changes to our program that switch it to them:

\
$ cat track/track.cxx

...
#include <libprotobuf/stream.hxx>

void
write (string_view name)
{
  ...

  s.string (1, name);

  ...

  cout.exceptions (std::ostream::badbit | std::ostream::failbit);

  protobuf::ostream os (cout);
  os.next ();
  os << m;
  os.close ();
}

void
read_track (const string& m)
{
  ... // As read() before.
}

void
read ()
{
  cin.exceptions (std::istream::badbit);

  protobuf::istream is (cin);

  while (is.next ())
    read_track (string (istreambuf_iterator<char> (is), {}));
}

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  try
  {
    if (argc == 2 && argv[1] == string_view (\"read\"))
      read ();
    else if (argc == 3 && argv[1] == string_view (\"write\"))
      write (argv[2]);
    else
    {
      println (cerr, \"usage: track read | track write <name>\");
      return 1;
    }
  }
  ... // As before.
}
\

On both sides \c{next()} begins the next message. What we then write to
\c{protobuf::ostream} makes up the message, which it writes to the other
stream preceded by its size when the next message begins or the stream is
closed. So the message is complete only after \c{close()}, which we call
explicitly (see \l{#contracts Programming Errors} for what happens if we
forget). The errors of writing to \c{cout} come through as exceptions,
which is why \c{protobuf::ostream} requires the other stream to have the
\c{badbit} and \c{failbit} exceptions enabled.

On the reading side, \c{next()} returns \c{false} once the other stream
ends. Otherwise, reading from \c{protobuf::istream} gets the bytes of the
message and then the end of the stream. The faults that it finds, such as a
stream that ends inside a message, come through as \c{invalid_input} as
well, whose position is then the offset of the message's size in the other
stream. This is why \c{protobuf::istream} requires the \c{badbit}
exception, which it enables by default.

Let's record two tracks and read them back:

\
$ track/track write 'morning run' >>tracks.bin
$ track/track write 'evening walk' >>tracks.bin

$ wc -c tracks.bin
79 tracks.bin

$ xxd tracks.bin | head -1
00000000: 260a 0b6d 6f72 6e69 6e67 2072 756e 1204  &..morning run..

$ track/track read <tracks.bin
name: morning run
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
point: -120 75
times: [0, 60, 125]
name: evening walk
point: 0 0
point: 3 -4
point: -120 75
times: [0, 60, 125]
\

As you can see, the first message is preceded by its size, \c{26} (38
bytes). The size comes from the stream, so \c{protobuf::istream} limits it
to keep a corrupt stream from making us wait for, and store, a message of
any size. The limit is 64MB unless we pass another one to the constructor.

\N|On Windows the standard streams are opened in the text mode, which
translates line endings and so corrupts binary data. A real \c{track} would
switch \c{stdin} and \c{stdout} to the binary mode with \c{_setmode()}
first.|


\h1#types|Schema Types|

The serializer and the parser have a function for each scalar type of the
Protocol Buffers language, which maps onto a wire type and a C++ type as
follows:

\
schema type        function          wire type  C++ type
-----------        --------          ---------  --------
int32              int32()           varint     int32_t
int64              int64()           varint     int64_t
uint32             uint32()          varint     uint32_t
uint64             uint64()          varint     uint64_t
sint32             sint32()          varint     int32_t
sint64             sint64()          varint     int64_t
bool               boolean()         varint     bool
enum               enumeration()     varint     int32_t
fixed32            fixed32()         i32        uint32_t
sfixed32           sfixed32()        i32        int32_t
fixed64            fixed64()         i64        uint64_t
sfixed64           sfixed64()        i64        int64_t
float              float32()         i32        float
double             float64()         i64        double
string             string()          len        string_view
bytes              bytes()           len        string_view
\

The functions of \c{bool}, \c{enum}, \c{float}, and \c{double} are named
after what they return, since the schema type names are C++ keywords. A
nested message and a group are written with \c{begin_message()} and
\c{begin_group()} up to the matching end function and read with
\c{message()} and \c{group()}, which return a parser of their fields.

The \c{int32}, \c{int64}, \c{uint32}, and \c{uint64} types write their value
as an unsigned varint, with a negative value as its 64-bit two's
complement. The parser takes a varint wider than the schema type as if
converting it to the type in C++, which keeps its lower bits, so a schema can
change a field between these four types. The \c{sint32} and \c{sint64} types
use ZigZag instead (see \l{#intro-write Writing a Message}). A \c{bool} is
written as 0 or 1 and read as \c{true} for any value other than 0. An
\c{enum} is an \c{int32}, which makes its values open: the parser returns
any value, and it is up to us to decide what to do with one that our schema
doesn't define.

The \c{string()} functions check that the value is valid UTF-8 as RFC 3629
defines it. The serializer throws \c{protobuf::invalid_output} before
writing anything and the parser throws \c{invalid_input}. The \c{bytes()}
functions take any value.


\h1#conformance|Conformance|

The parser accepts every message that the encoding specification defines and
rejects everything else. Specifically, it throws \c{invalid_input} on the
first of the following faults in a field that it reads:

\ul|

\li|A key that ends with the input or is wider than 32 bits.|

\li|Field number 0, which is reserved.|

\li|Wire types 6 and 7, which the encoding doesn't define.|

\li|A varint that ends with the input or holds more than 64 bits, which is
the case when its tenth byte holds more than bit 63.|

\li|An \c{i32} or \c{i64} value that ends with the input.|

\li|A \c{len} value whose length goes past the end of the input.|

\li|An \c{egroup} key without a matching \c{sgroup} key before it, a group
without its \c{egroup} key, and an \c{egroup} key of another field than its
group's.|

|

And when a value is read as a schema type:

\ul|

\li|A wire type that doesn't match the schema type. For a repeated field,
\c{len} matches as well, since it holds the packed elements.|

\li|A packed element that ends with its field.|

\li|A \c{string} value that is not valid UTF-8.|

|

Note that the parser only checks the value of a \c{len} field as a nested
message or packed elements once we read it as such, since only the schema
tells what it holds. A group, on the other hand, the parser has to step
over, so it finds its end as soon as it gets to it. The groups nested in it
are checked once we read the group with \c{group()}.

The parser accepts the varints that have more bytes than their value needs
(\c{80 00} for 0, say), which the encoding allows. The reference
implementation also accepts a tenth byte with more than bit 63 and drops the
bits that don't fit, which we reject since no 64-bit value is written that
way.

The serializer only writes messages that the parser accepts. It writes a
negative \c{int32} sign-extended to 64 bits, as the encoding requires, so
that a reader of the field as \c{int64} gets the same value, and it leaves
out a packed field without elements, which adds nothing to its repeated
field.


\h1#contracts|Programming Errors|

Using the library in a way that its documentation rules out is a programming
error, which the library reports as a contract violation. Recall that in the
last version of \c{track} we call \c{close()} on \c{protobuf::ostream}
explicitly. Let's see what happens if we forget:

\
$ track/track write 'morning run' >/dev/null
stream.cxx:274: error: precondition violated: !is_open () ||
    !good () || uncaught_exceptions () != 0
stream.cxx:274: note: in '~ostream'
\

A \c{protobuf::ostream} that is destroyed while still open would lose its
last message, so it treats this as a programming error unless the stream is
no longer good or an exception is being thrown. Similarly, a field number
outside of the range from 1 to \c{protobuf::max_field} and an
\c{end_message()} call without a message to end are contract violations.

By default, a contract violation prints a report like this to \c{stderr} and
aborts the process. To handle it differently, for example, to throw an
exception in a test, we can install our own handler with
\c{protobuf::set_contract_handler()}, declared in
\c{<libprotobuf/contract.hxx>}. The handler receives a
\c{protobuf::contract_violation} that describes the failed check. If the
handler returns, the default report is printed and the process is aborted
all the same.

The amount of checking is selected when building the library with the
\c{LIBPROTOBUF_CONTRACT} macro: \c{0} omits the checks, \c{1} (the default)
checks preconditions and assertions, and \c{2} adds postconditions and
invariants. For example, to run the library's tests at the highest level:

\
$ b test config.cxx.poptions=-DLIBPROTOBUF_CONTRACT=2
\
"
